  <section class="doc-section">
    <p class="eyebrow">Experimental lifecycle baseline</p>
    <h2>Every modality gets the same safety checkpoints.</h2>
    <p>The experimental <code>ModalityLifecycle</code> is deliberately separate from the supported tabular <code>MLProject</code> path. It runs ingestion, validation, profiling, curation, leakage-aware splitting, bounded feature preparation, modelling, evaluation, data and model explanations, signed packaging, verified inference, monitoring, documentation, and recovery checks through one polymorphic runner. Its optional <code>ModalityManifest</code> binds source, lineage, privacy, task, and curation evidence to the exact content hash before training. Its explanation gate independently reconstructs the linear decision, checks zero-ablation direction, and measures small feature-space perturbation stability; it does not turn a representation-space result into raw-modality causality.</p>
    <p>That common contract makes the comparison honest. A WAV baseline reports signal statistics; a video baseline reports probed metadata unless the caller supplies a bounded frame decoder, in which case it reports evenly sampled frame statistics; a graph baseline uses declared node features without cross-partition topology; and a geospatial baseline uses geometry envelopes in the declared CRS. None silently turns a foundation into a production representation model.</p>
    <div class="callout"><p>Use this path to validate data policy, evidence lineage, package integrity, and baseline behaviour. Promote a modality only after its model-specific representation, serving, resource, and independent-review evidence is attached.</p></div>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-section">
    <p class="eyebrow">Reference serving</p>
    <h2>Bring the decoder. Keep the boundary visible.</h2>
    <p><code>ModalityHttpServing</code> exposes a signed <code>ModalityPredictor</code> through <code>/health</code>, <code>/schema</code>, and <code>/predict</code>. The core deliberately does not guess how a PDF, image, audio stream, graph, or geospatial object should be decoded. Supply a <code>ModalitySampleDecoder</code> that owns that policy, validates its payload, and returns the adapter's sample type.</p>
    <pre><code>{`class DocumentDecoder(ModalitySampleDecoder):
    def decode(self, payload):
        return tuple(DocumentSample.from_dict(item)
                     for item in payload["samples"])

    def schema(self):
        return {"required": ["samples"], "sample_fields": ["content"]}

serving = ModalityHttpServing(predictor, DocumentDecoder())
serving.start()`}</code></pre>
    <p>The server bounds body size and sample count, verifies the package before prediction, and returns structured request errors. It is a reference transport: put it behind authentication, TLS, request tracing, redaction, and an owned operational gateway before claiming production service coverage.</p>
  </section>
  <p class="meta">* The supported claim is intentionally narrow: the explicitly gated tabular library workflow, not every possible tabular source or model.</p>

  <h2>The lifecycle gate</h2>
  <figure class="diagram">
    <figcaption class="diagram-caption"><span>SUPPORT DECISION</span><strong>One missing stage keeps the claim below supported.</strong></figcaption>
    <div class="flow-row">
      <div class="flow-node"><span>01</span><b>Declare</b><small>modality · task · scope</small></div>
      <i class="flow-arrow">→</i>
      <div class="flow-node"><span>02</span><b>Evidence</b><small>tests · artifacts · failures</small></div>
      <i class="flow-arrow">→</i>
      <div class="flow-node active"><span>03</span><b>Review</b><small>independent decision</small></div>
      <i class="flow-arrow">→</i>
      <div class="flow-node"><span>04</span><b>Publish</b><small>label · limits · owner</small></div>
    </div>
  </figure>
  <p>Declarations are checked before training. They name required extras, resources, known failure modes, limitations, and evidence links. An incomplete declaration cannot be promoted by calling it experimental; the missing stages remain visible.</p>

  <h2>What to add when expanding support</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Start from raw fixtures: valid, empty, corrupt, oversized, shifted, duplicated, leaked, and privacy-sensitive inputs.</li>
    <li>Implement the modality-neutral contracts first: snapshot identity, curation findings, split identity, lineage, release evaluation, and explanation metadata.</li>
    <li>Run the same 15-stage lifecycle: ingestion, validation, profiling, curation, splitting, feature preparation, modelling, evaluation, data explainability, model explainability, packaging, inference, monitoring, documentation, and recovery/resource tests.</li>
    <li>Only then change the capability label, and attach the command, artifact, digest, reviewer, and limitations to the evidence index.</li>
  </ol>

  <div class="callout"><p><strong>Contributor rule:</strong> if a new model works only because the data contract is bypassed, it is not an expansion of support. It is an unreviewed exception.</p></div>
